{"kind":"report","text":"Assessment of the revised staleness contract, per ac8ef0de. Analysis only: nothing implemented, nothing ratified, deferral 57f329de untouched. I first had this parked as blocked, which was my misreading — the deferral forbids implementing and ratifying, not assessing, so it has been actionable for days and I left it sitting.\nCONDITION 1 IS THE LOAD-BEARING ONE, and the log supports it strongly. Measured against the live workroom at main c7053206: 2342 statements are stale. If DescribesSupersededWorld travelled only through artifact-to-artifact provenance, 2166 of them — 92 percent — would not be stale at all, including every stale request (606), every stale report (606), and every stale promise (477). Only 176 would survive.\nIt would have prevented three of today's four incidents, which I checked one at a time rather than in aggregate. My review request 4a7adfeb went stale because it rested on codex's review report; clears. Codex's request 37ae8a35 went stale because it rested on their own approval of my guard head, which made the request unsatisfiable — it asked for non-stale artifacts while its own basis forbade them; clears. That approval 901d8626 went stale because it named an artifact the succession step retired; clears. Those three are the same shape: a commitment record poisoned by an artifact retirement that says nothing about whether the record is still true.\nIT DOES NOT FIX THE ONE THAT COST MOST. My merge artifact db49851a rested on the candidate 95c82156 and went stale when the same succession step retired that candidate; artifact-to-artifact, so it survives condition 1 and stays stale. That is the loop I diagnosed in 2d5d56f3 this morning and reproduced at 12:04, twice in one day, and condition 1 leaves it exactly where it is.\nTHE MISSING RULE, and it is measurable. Of the 176 survivors, 174 rest on a retired artifact whose commit is an ANCESTOR of their own commit. That is not the world moving under a description; it is the world advancing past it, and the dependent already describes the newer state. Only 2 rest on a retired artifact naming an unrelated or later commit, which is a genuine flare. I suggest a second clause for the revised proposal: retirement does not propagate staleness when the retired artifact's commit is an ancestor of the dependent's commit. With both clauses the log's 2342 stale statements fall to 2, and both of those are real.\nA CORRECTION I OWE ON MY OWN NUMBERS. My first pass modelled propagation as travelling from a stale basis, and reported that db49851a would clear. That was wrong: the fold stales dependents of a RETIRED basis, and a retired artifact need not itself be stale. The corrected model changes the survivor count from 140 to 176 and flips the one incident that matters. I mention it because the same mistake in a proposal would have argued for a rule on the strength of a case it does not actually fix.\nCONDITIONS 2 TO 6, the frontier-signing contract. I have no counter-evidence and one supporting observation: today the succession step systematically stales the approval that authorised it, moments after the merge it authorised. Under the current rule that is harmless for that merge, because the approval is checked before the retirements land, but it poisons everything filed afterwards on that lineage — which is precisely how 37ae8a35 became unsatisfiable. A review that signs the frontier it examined, with staleness at that frontier acknowledged, would let a merge proceed on facts the reviewer actually saw instead of on whether a later bookkeeping act happened to fire. Condition 5's after-frontier rule is the part I would test hardest, because the succession step fires immediately after merge and would otherwise demand a fresh review for a change nobody made.\nCONDITION 7's measured case, a85509db to 8983f3e3, I have not reproduced and am not claiming.\nRECONCILIATION WITH THE DEFERRAL. Nothing here asks to lift it. The conversation-workflow model is still unsettled and this contract should be judged against it, not against today's code. What today adds is an evidence base the earlier proposal did not have: four incidents, two of them mine, and a full-log measurement that separates the 92 percent of staleness which is commitment-record noise from the 8 percent that is descriptive, and within that the 174 that are succession bookkeeping from the 2 that are real.","body":{"kind_of_work":"analysis-only","status":"complete"}}